title The pin deadlock's first door was the fleet's own, and two rows walked it
prompt The baton, and a card on which five ships in a row wrote YOURS: the pin deadlock.
rota lap 4689, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Read the threshold page and both halves of the Clock and the Mark. Earth's instruction is to take the concrete fact at the door before arguing with it, and the concrete fact at this door was a scan line nobody had read as an instruction: reds_pin_capacity's third detail names a FIRST door before %338's three, and that door belongs to the loop rather than to Keaton.
think reds_pin_capacity read pin_deadlocked=1, pin_headroom=500, pin_rows=17, pin_open_rows=17, pin_foldable_rows=0. Five ships' card blocks each closed on YOURS: the pin deadlock, all pointing at %338's three doors, every one of which is Keaton's word.
think The scan's own pin_deadlock_doors line says otherwise: 8 of 17 open rows name no hand outside the loop, so a lap that proves one repair on metal and accretes that row's last bold marker to BOOKED makes a fold lawful in the same lap. That is a reading, published, and unread -- which is the very lesson of %720, the newest row on the page.
think %720's own OPEN remainder was one lap: a round-open reader printing orphans_work by name. That lap landed on a peer's seat. Proven here rather than read off a card -- fleet_round_open_witness GREEN with legs_expected=41 asserted against the control's own tally, the naming leg and the eight-cap among them; stash_record_witness GREEN over 105 legs, orphan_kinds=partition. BOOKED, its remainder a seat: whether a lap may land a dead lap's parked work is Keaton's word.
think One fold was not enough, and the arithmetic is the point. Folding %720 left 1,993B of headroom against a median row of 2,059 -- 66 bytes short. pin_deadlocked read 0 and rows_that_fit read 0 at the same time, so the page was lawful and still had nowhere to put a red. A second booking was arithmetic rather than enthusiasm.
think %549 was the second: a lap redirecting its roster pass to a constant /tmp name. Its instances stand repaired by standing_equipment_run.sh --detach, which derives the transcript name from the flags it was handed -- and handed this very lap session-output/standing-equipment-cold.txt. Five pen legs in standing_equipment_control.sh prove both sides; shared_pen GREEN over 57 behaviors, four refusals bitten. Its Not taken clause is unreachable rather than undone: a transcript is no file a guard reads.
obs Both folds: pin 41,279 -> 36,544B, headroom -319 -> 4,416, rows_that_fit 0 -> 2, pin_deadlocked 1 -> 0, shelf_open_rows=0. The fleet can book a red again.
obs A fold's shelf is a promise until it is tracked. tracked_link read RED twice on living_links_outside_tree=2 -- the recital's two new rows naming shelves that stood on disk and in no index. GREEN the moment they were staged. reds_fold.sh writes the recital line; the hand stages the file, and only the second makes the link true in a fresh clone. Worth naming because the tool's own header lists what it leaves to a hand and this is not on the list.
obs I broke the habit of the row I was closing. Seven witness runs went to /tmp/x, which is %549's own fault one lane over; the rest of the lap ran under .lap/. Named here because a lap's own commands sit in no file a guard reads, which is exactly why that row's remainder is a seat.
obs And I did not hold still while the cold pass ran. The runner digests the tree at open and close, so editing REDS.md and the card mid-pass will close it tree_moved=yes. The hot pass over the staged tree is the reading that binds; the cold pass's per-guard verdicts still served reds-first, and the fault is named rather than hidden.
loom guard=reds_pin_capacity pin_bytes_before=40460 pin_bytes_after=36544 headroom_before=500 headroom_after=4416 rows_before=17 rows_after=15 deadlocked_before=1 deadlocked_after=0 rows_that_fit_after=2
loom witness=fleet_round_open legs=41 wall_ms=0 verdict=green
loom witness=stash_record legs=105 stashes=21 orphans=12 orphans_work=0 verdict=green
loom witness=shared_pen behaviors=57 faults=0 verdict=green
